---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Hallucination & Verification - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="hallucination">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI models make things up. Not occasionally but regularly. They'll invent API endpoints that
				don't exist, cite papers that were never written, reference function parameters that aren't
				real, and generate code that calls libraries with the wrong interface. They do all of this
				with complete confidence, in the same tone they use when they're correct.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is called <strong class="text-stone-800">hallucination</strong>, and it's one of the largest problems in this space. It's a fundamental property of how these models work.
				They generate text that fits the pattern, and sometimes the most plausible-sounding next word
				happens to be wrong. The model has no mechanism to check its own output against reality.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Where Hallucinations Hit Hardest
			</h3>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">API details and library usage</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						The model might know that a library exists and roughly what it does, but get the exact
						method names, parameter order, or return types wrong. This is especially common with
						newer libraries or less popular ones where training data is sparse. It may also
						confidently use an older version of an API that has since changed. It can even invent
						whole package names that don't exist, which carries a security risk (attackers register
						the hallucinated names with malicious code). We cover that in <a href="/development/common-limitations">Common Limitations</a>.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Outdated information</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Models have a training data cutoff date. They don't know about changes, updates, or new
						releases that happened after that date. A model might tell you to use a deprecated
						method, reference an old configuration format, or miss a breaking change in a recent
						major version. It won't tell you its information might be stale. It doesn't know.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Specific facts and statistics
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Numbers, dates, proper names, URLs: anything specific and verifiable is high-risk for
						hallucination. The model generates what sounds plausible, not what's accurate. A
						statistic might be in the right ballpark or completely fabricated. You can't tell from
						the output alone.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">Your own codebase</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Unless the model can actually see your code (via tools like Cursor or Claude Code), it
						will guess at your file structure, function names, and conventions. These guesses are
						often plausible enough to look correct at a glance but wrong in specifics.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="tip">
				<p>
					The model doesn't know what it doesn't know. It can't tell you "I'm not sure about this"
					reliably. When it says "I'm not sure," it's generating that phrase because
					the pattern fits, not because it has genuine uncertainty. Treat all AI output as
					unverified until you've checked it yourself.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Always Verify
			</h3>

			<p>
				The only reliable defense against hallucination is verification. This doesn't mean you need
				to fact-check every word; that would make AI tools useless. It means developing a sense for
				what's safe to trust and what needs checking.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="tip" title="Run & Test the Code">
					<p>
						The most reliable verification for generated code is running it. Does it compile? Do
						the tests pass? Does it actually do what it's supposed to? This catches hallucinated
						APIs, wrong parameter types, and logic errors faster than reading ever will.
					</p>
					<p>
						Make sure to test all the edge cases and scenarios that the model may have missed.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Check the documentation">
					<p>
						When the model suggests using a specific API, method, or configuration, verify it
						against the official docs. This takes seconds and catches the most common type of
						hallucination. Better yet, include a link to the relevant documentation in your prompt
						so the model has the correct information to work from.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Provide source material">
					<p>
						The best way to prevent hallucination is to give the model the actual information it
						needs. Paste in the relevant documentation. Link to the existing code. Include the
						actual error message. When the model can reference real data instead of generating from
						patterns, accuracy improves dramatically.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Be skeptical of specifics">
					<p>
						General concepts and well-known patterns are usually reliable. Specific version numbers,
						exact API signatures, precise statistics, and URLs are high-risk. Apply more scrutiny
						to specifics and you'll catch most problems.
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<h3>
				Tips for Reducing Hallucination
			</h3>

			<div class="space-y-3 my-8">
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Link to documentation</strong> in your prompts. If
						you're working with a specific API, paste in the relevant docs or give the model access
						to them. This grounds its responses in reality. You can also add these to skills, rules, or other documentation where appropriate.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Point to existing code (golden masters)</strong> rather than asking the
						model to guess your conventions. "Follow the pattern in
						src/services/userService.ts" is far better than "write a service."
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Ask it to cite sources</strong> when dealing with
						factual claims. The model may still hallucinate citations, but asking forces it to
						consider whether it actually has a source, and gives you something to verify by clicking and reading a link.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Ask it to search the web</strong>. If you're working
						with anything released or updated recently, assume the model's information might be
						outdated and either provide current docs or ask it to search the web for the information.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
					<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-orange-400 shrink-0 mt-2"></div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-stone-800">Use tools that can verify</strong>. Claude Code can
						actually run code and check results. Cursor can read your real files. Tools with access
						to ground truth produce far fewer hallucinations than a plain chat window.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="The practical takeaway">
				<p>
					Hallucination isn't going away. It's a fundamental trade-off of the technology. But it's
					manageable. Provide real context, verify critical output, and develop a healthy skepticism
					for specific claims. The model is an excellent assistant, but it's like a new employee: it's your job to catch its
					mistakes. And remember: it won't remember your corrections next conversation. Each new
					chat starts from scratch.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
